[38;2;124;124;124mSo the functional is $\int_0^1 \kappa(s)^2 \, ds$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor a circle of circumference 1, the radius is $r = \frac{1}{2\pi}$, and the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcurvature is $\kappa = \frac{1}{r} = 2\pi$, constant. So the integral is $[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\int_0^1 (2\pi)^2 ds = 4\pi^2$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow, this is a classic problem in the calculus of variations / differential [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mgeometry. The question is whether the circle minimizes $\int \kappa^2 \, ds$ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mamong all closed non-self-intersecting curves of fixed length.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis is related to the "elastic energy" or "bending energy" of a curve. The [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfunctional $\int \kappa^2 \, ds$ is the bending energy.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor closed curves, there's a well-known result. Let me think about whether the [0m
